extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Axes and gridlines"
	- pagedescription = "Styling the axes, the numbers and titles along them, and the gridlines in Tablechart."
	- const varieties = [['Elstar', '62', '1.20'], ['Jonagold', '75', '1.05'], ['Braeburn', '48', '1.45'], ['Gala', '55', '1.30'], ['Cox', '35', '1.85'], ['Discovery', '30', '1.75']]

mixin varieties
	table
		thead
			tr
				th Variety
				th Harvest per tree (kg)
				th Price per kg (€)
		tbody
			each row in varieties
				tr
					th= row[0]
					td= row[1]
					td= row[2]

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Axes and gridlines
				p.lead The axes, the numbers and titles along them, and the gridlines.

	+section("Where they are")
		p A #[a(href=url('/types/scatter.html')) scatter chart] always has an axis on the left and one at the bottom, with a short mark at each number. On the other types the value axis is off by default: #[code data-chart-axis] adds its numbers, on the left, or under a bar chart. On every type but the donut, #[code data-chart-grid] adds the gridlines. The #[code axis] and #[code grid] options do the same for every chart.
		p This is how they look without styling of your own.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				+varieties

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b Rainfall at the orchard
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p A gridline stops a little before each number and name on the chart, and goes on after it. The gridline is cut out there, so the text reads on any background.
		p Set the variables on the chart, on a class or on #[code :root], as other variables.

	+section("The scale")
		p With an axis or gridlines, the scale ends on a number of the axis: a chart with 84 as its highest value and steps of 10 goes up to 90. #[code data-chart-scale] sets the scale yourself. Two numbers are where it starts and ends, and Tablechart chooses the numbers between them. Three numbers or more are the numbers along the axis.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid data-chart-scale="0 25 50 75 100")
				figcaption: b Apples picked, out of 100
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p A value beyond the scale you set is still shown whole: the scale goes on until it. On a column, bar, line or area chart the scale always includes zero. On a scatter chart it does not, and #[code data-chart-scale-x] sets the axis across.

	+section("The variables")
		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-color
					td The lines of the axes: the text colour at 55% by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-width
					td The width of the lines of the axes: #[code 1px] by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-axis-size
					td The size of the numbers and titles along the axes: the size of the names by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-color
					td The colour of the gridlines: the text colour by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-opacity
					td The opacity of the gridlines: #[code 0.09] by default
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-grid-width
					td The width of the gridlines: #[code 1px] by default

	+section("Softer axes, stronger gridlines")
		p With lighter axes, the gridlines can be stronger, so the reader can follow a marker to its number.

		+style.
			.soft-axes {
				--tablechart-axis-color: color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 25%, transparent);
				--tablechart-grid-opacity: 0.25;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.soft-axes(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				+varieties

	+section("Dashed gridlines")
		p The gridlines are one #[code path] with the class #[code tablechart-grid], so CSS can set more than the variables, such as dashes. A dash is in pixels, at any size of the chart.

		+style.
			.dashed-grid .tablechart-grid {
				stroke-dasharray: 1 3;
				opacity: 0.4;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.dashed-grid(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				+varieties

	+section("The numbers and titles")
		p #[code --tablechart-axis-size] sets the size of the numbers along the axes and of the titles. The numbers have the class #[code tablechart-tick], the titles #[code tablechart-axis-title], so they can also be styled on their own.

		+style.
			.small-axes {
				--tablechart-axis-size: 0.8em;
			}
			.small-axes .tablechart-axis-title {
				font-weight: 600;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.small-axes(data-chart="scatter")
				+varieties

		p A chart with smaller numbers has room for more of them, so it may show more steps.

	+section("Light and dark")
		p The axes and gridlines use the text colour by default, so they follow the colour scheme. To set a colour for each scheme, use #[code light-dark()]. See #[a(href=url('/styling/light-dark.html')) Light and dark].

		+codeblock('CSS').
			:root {
				--tablechart-grid-color: light-dark(#16323f, #f3efe9);
				--tablechart-axis-color: light-dark(#8aa0aa, #5b6f78);
			}

	+section("Next")
		p See #[a(href=url('/reference/variables.html')) CSS variables] for all variables.
